  "content": {
    "id": "p6-q7",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Show that the triangle with vertices $A(1,0)$, $B(6,5)$ and $C(0,2)$ is right-angled by:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p6-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Using Pythagoras’ theorem.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$AB^2=50$, $BC^2=45$, $CA^2=5$. Since $50=45+5$, the triangle is right-angled at $C$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}AB^2&=(6-1)^2+(5-0)^2\\\\AB^2&=50\\\\BC^2&=(0-6)^2+(2-5)^2\\\\BC^2&=45\\\\CA^2&=(1-0)^2+(0-2)^2\\\\CA^2&=5\\\\BC^2+CA^2&=45+5\\\\BC^2+CA^2&=50\\\\AB^2&=BC^2+CA^2\\end{align*}$$\nBy the converse of Pythagoras’ theorem, the triangle is right-angled at $C$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 75
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Using the gradients of two sides.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$m_{BC}=\\frac12$ and $m_{CA}=-2$. Their product is $-1$, so $BC\\perp CA$ and the triangle is right-angled at $C$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{BC}&=\\frac{2-5}{0-6}\\\\m_{BC}&=\\frac12\\\\m_{CA}&=\\frac{0-2}{1-0}\\\\m_{CA}&=-2\\\\m_{BC}\\times m_{CA}&=\\frac12\\times(-2)\\\\m_{BC}\\times m_{CA}&=-1\\end{align*}$$\nThe gradients are negative reciprocals, so $BC\\perp CA$. The triangle is right-angled at $C$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
